<!-- ==================================================================
     KHAATA — BUILD LOG
     Sprint 43 — Deeper Rule-Based Intelligence, Part 2 of 3 (proactive
     post-save insights, App.Chat — additive only, no external AI API)
       App version bumped: 1.37.0 -> 1.38.0
       Goal: after a transaction is added via voice/chat, have the
       assistant proactively mention ONE short, genuinely useful insight
       when there's something real to say — e.g. "this puts you close to
       your Food budget", "this is unusually high for this category",
       "you're on track with your goal" — reusing the EXISTING
       App.Advisor/App.Budget/App.Analytics engines exactly as they
       already compute things for the Advisor tab and the manual entry
       form's real-time toast. No new financial math was written this
       sprint; everything below only reads numbers those engines already
       produce.
       What's new:
         - App.Advisor.Notifications.computeAlerts(txn) — the duplicate/
           unusually-large/over-budget/low-balance checks that already
           lived inline inside onTransactionSaved() were extracted into
           this new pure function (each alert also gained a stable `id`,
           used only for the new chat cooldown below). onTransactionSaved()
           itself now just calls computeAlerts() and toasts the results —
           byte-for-byte the same behavior as before for the manual entry
           form; #txnForm's real-time toast is completely unchanged.
         - Inside the App.Chat IIFE: proactiveExpenseInsight(txn) calls
           that same computeAlerts(txn) first (so a chat-added expense
           gets the identical duplicate/large/over-budget/low-balance
           checks the manual form already gets). If nothing there fires,
           it falls back to reading App.Advisor.Analysis.patterns()
           .budgetUsage — the exact figures App.Advisor.Budget.suggestions()
           already uses for the Advisor tab — against the same existing
           BUDGET_NEAR_LIMIT_PCT threshold, to catch "close to budget but
           not over yet". No budget/average/percentage math was
           reimplemented; both paths only read what Analysis/Notifications
           already compute.
         - proactiveGoalInsight(goalId) — for a chat-logged goal
           contribution, calls the existing computeGoalPlan()/
           computeGoalStatus() (same functions the Goals tab already uses)
           and, only for a real 'On Track' or 'Behind' status, returns one
           short sentence.
         - saveDraft()'s income/expense branch now also calls
           App.Advisor.Manager.run() after saving (same refresh the manual
           form already triggers, so the Advisor tab stays in sync with
           chat-added transactions too), then appends at most one insight
           sentence — from proactiveExpenseInsight() — as a second
           paragraph onto the existing "Done ✅ —…" confirmation string.
           saveDraft()'s savings/goal-contribution branch does the same
           with proactiveGoalInsight(). No other branch (transfer, loan,
           investment, budget, goal-creation, reminder) was touched, and
           the confirmation text itself is unchanged when there's nothing
           worth flagging — insightText is simply appended when present,
           never replacing anything.
         - New small chat-only cooldown (insightLog, persisted under
           localStorage key 'khaata:chatinsightlog', loaded/saved by the
           existing restore()/persist() pair) stops the SAME insight id
           from repeating within 12 hours, so the assistant doesn't nag
           about the same thing on every single message — this is UI/flow
           gating only, not a financial calculation, and is independent of
           the Advisor tab's own separate 14-day dismiss cooldown
           (App.Advisor.Logger/state.advisor.dismissed), which is left
           completely untouched.
         - Because the insight text is appended into the very string
           saveDraft() already returns, and that string is what
           App.Voice.Bridge's aiMessageListeners already speak aloud
           (Sprint 19), voice conversations get the same proactive insight
           read out with zero changes to any voice code.
       No IDs, classes, navigation, or existing feature were touched.
       Part 3 (next sprint) will add natural conversational variety/tone
       polish plus broader open-ended follow-up handling within the
       rule-based engine.

     Sprint 42 — Deeper Rule-Based Intelligence, Part 1 of 3 (situation
     recognition, App.Chat — additive only, no external AI API)
       App version bumped: 1.36.0 -> 1.37.0
       Goal: recognize more everyday money situations by natural Hindi/
       English/Hinglish phrasing — got a bonus, got a gift, salary hike,
       lent money, borrowed money, got a refund, paid someone back —
       and reply with a short natural-language sentence that shows
       understanding, instead of a generic "Should I save this as ₹X
       Other income?" prompt. Everything stays 100% local pattern-
       matching, same spirit as App.Chat's existing classifyKind().
       Reused as-is, NOT rebuilt: App.Chat's classifyKind()/
       matchKindWord() (still the only classifier for every message
       this sprint doesn't specifically recognize), the whole slot-
       filling machinery (nextMissingStep/questionFor/askNextQuestion/
       applyAnswerToStep/askConfirmation), extractInitialFields(), and
       App.VoiceBrain/App.VoiceFollowUp (both already delegate every
       add_transaction voice turn straight to this same App.Chat code
       path, so voice gets this for free with zero changes there).
       What's new (all additive, inside the App.Chat IIFE):
         - detectSituation(t) — a small pre-check that runs BEFORE
           classifyKind() in startFlowFromMessage(). It only returns a
           result for a handful of specific, high-confidence phrasings;
           everything else returns null and falls straight through to
           the original classifyKind()/matchKindWord() path, completely
           unchanged. Recognizes: bonus, salary hike/raise/increment,
           refund/cashback, a *received* gift (deliberately excludes
           "bought a gift for..." so it never poaches a real expense),
           lending money, borrowing money, and — the one real
           misclassification bug fixed here — "paid X back" / "repaid" /
           "chuka diya" / "wapas kar diya", which classifyKind() alone
           would read as a generic expense (it contains the word
           "paid") even though it's really someone settling an existing
           loan, not a new expense.
         - DEFAULT_INCOME_SOURCES gained two new default categories,
           "Bonus" and "Refund", so the existing findCategoryInText()
           category-matching (already used by extractInitialFields())
           picks these up with zero new matching code.
         - nextMissingStep()/questionFor() gained one additive branch
           each for the loan kind's new f.isRepayment flag — skips the
           normal "did you give or take this loan?" question (moot for
           a repayment) and asks who was paid back instead. Every
           existing loan (new-loan) path is untouched.
         - buildSummaryText() now checks f.__situation first and, only
           when set, returns a natural confirmation sentence (e.g.
           "Samajh gaya — ₹5000 ka bonus, income mein add kar doon?")
           via the new situationSummaryText() instead of the generic
           phrasing. No f.__situation means the original switch/case
           text runs exactly as before.
         - saveDraft()'s loan branch gained an isRepayment path that
           looks for a matching existing open debt (same person, same
           direction) and logs a payment against it — reusing the exact
           same payment-record shape the manual "Log Payment" form
           (dpayBtn) already writes — marking it settled if fully paid.
           If no matching debt is found, it falls back to saving a
           standalone settled debt record (so the repayment is still
           visible on the Debts screen) rather than silently forcing it
           into Income/Expense or dropping it. Brand-new loans (no
           repayment phrasing detected) save exactly as before.
       No IDs, classes, navigation, or existing feature were touched.
       #micFabBtn's Sprint 41 behavior is untouched. Part 2 (next
       sprint) will add proactive insight-style replies during the
       conversation — e.g. "this is close to your budget limit" — by
       reusing App.Advisor/App.Budget/App.Analytics, not rebuilding
       them.

     Sprint 41 — Mic FAB -> Real Conversation (no-op wiring fix)
       App version bumped: 1.35.0 -> 1.36.0
       Problem: tapping the floating mic button on the Home screen
       (#micFabBtn) opened the OLD, simple "Voice Assistant" sheet
       (#voiceOverlay — single-shot commands only, from Sprint 19's
       predecessor sprints), requiring a second tap on "Start Listening"
       (#voiceStartBtn) inside that sheet before anything happened. Every
       OTHER mic entry point in the app — the "AI Voice Assistant
       (Conversational)" card's mic (#veMicBtn) and the chat panel's mic
       (#chatMicBtn), both added in Sprint 19 — already started a real,
       back-and-forth conversation with one tap via
       App.Voice.Bridge.startConversation(). This sprint makes the FAB
       consistent with those two.
       What changed (everything else untouched):
         - #micFabBtn's click handler no longer calls
           openVoicePanel()/startListening(). It now calls
           App.Voice.startConversation() / App.Voice.stopConversation()
           — the exact same Bridge functions #veMicBtn and #chatMicBtn
           already call — with the same active-conversation toggle
           behavior (tap again to stop). This automatically gets
           everything Bridge.startConversation() already does: opens the
           chat panel if closed, pauses App.Voice.WakeWord (Sprint 40)
           for the duration and resumes it after via
           stopConversation() -> WakeWord.resumeIfEnabled(), resets any
           stale App.VoiceFollowUp question (Sprint 38), and reuses the
           existing 8-state Recognition/Synthesis/Manager machine — no
           new orchestration logic was written for this sprint. A
           feature-detect guard falls back to the old
           openVoicePanel()/startListening() behavior only if
           App.Voice.startConversation isn't available for some reason
           (e.g. a stale cached page), so the button is never a dead tap.
         - App.Voice.UI.renderState() (the same function that already
           drives #veMicBtn's live listening glow off the real state
           machine) gained one additive line so #micFabBtn's existing
           .mic-fab.listening CSS glow (Sprint 29/30) now also reflects
           the real conversation state, the same way #veMicBtn's does.
         - Old single-shot flow preserved 100% as-is: openVoicePanel(),
           closeVoicePanel(), startListening(), stopListening(),
           updateMicButtons(), #voiceOverlay, #voiceStartBtn,
           #voiceTranscriptBox, #voiceTextInput, and everything else in
           that module — zero lines changed. Since #micFabBtn was its
           only entry point before this sprint, one new button — "🎙️
           Open Quick Voice Commands" (#voiceOldPanelOpenBtn) — was
           added to the existing "🎙️ Voice Assistant" settings card on
           the Voice Assistant tab/more-item, calling the same untouched
           openVoicePanel(). This keeps the single-shot sheet fully
           reachable exactly as promised, without it living behind the
           FAB anymore.
       No other IDs, classes, navigation, views, or features were added,
       removed, or altered.
     Sprint 40 — Voice Command Brain, Part 4 of 4 (Custom Assistant Name
     + Wake-Word Listening — App.Voice.WakeWord new)
       App version bumped: 1.34.0 -> 1.35.0
       Goal: let the user name their assistant, and optionally turn on
       hands-free "Hey <name>" wake-word listening so the existing
       tap-to-talk flow (Sprints 19/37/38/39) can also start itself when
       it hears its own name — without ever replacing the mic button.

       What already existed (checked first, reused as-is):
         - App.Voice.Recognition/Manager/Bridge — the real Web Speech
           API listening session, state machine, and
           startConversation()/stopConversation() orchestration every
           previous voice sprint already built. Untouched.
         - App.Voice.Config.SETTINGS_DEFAULTS already reserved a
           `wakeWord: false` flag and the UI already showed a "Wake
           Word" tag under "reserved for future sprints" (Sprint 19) —
           this sprint is what makes that flag and that promise real,
           reusing the same key rather than introducing a new one.
         - App.Voice.Settings (get/set/persist to localStorage),
           App.Voice.Permission.request()/check(), App.Voice.Language.
           resolveLocale(), App.Voice.Logger/Events — all reused as-is.
         - The "🧩 AI Voice Assistant (Conversational)" card (#veCard)
           already had a toggle-row/switch pattern for Auto-speak and
           Continuous listening — the new controls below follow the
           exact same markup pattern, not a new one.

       New: App.Voice.Config.SETTINGS_DEFAULTS.assistantName (default
       'Khaata') — the custom name typed into the new "Assistant's
       name" field, persisted the same way every other App.Voice.
       Settings key already is (khaata:voiceEngineSettings).

       New module App.Voice.WakeWord (passive, secondary listener):
         - Builds its OWN separate `continuous:true` SpeechRecognition
           instance — deliberately not reusing App.Voice.Recognition's
           singleton, so passive wake-word listening can never corrupt
           the real conversation's 8-state machine (App.Voice.Config.
           STATE_TRANSITIONS is untouched and unaware this module
           exists).
         - While active, checks every interim/final result for the
           user's chosen assistantName as a whole-word match (simple
           normalize + regex — no NLP, same "pure/placeholder-free but
           deliberately simple" spirit as App.Voice.Language.
           resolveLocale()). On a match: stops itself
           (pauseForConversation()) and calls the REAL
           App.Voice.Bridge.startConversation() — the exact same
           function the manual mic button already calls, so a wake-word
           trigger behaves identically to a tap.
         - enable()/disable() persist `wakeWord` via the existing
           App.Voice.Settings.set(), and enable() requests mic
           permission via the existing App.Voice.Permission.request()
           if it isn't already granted — never a silent background
           permission grab.
         - Auto-restart with backoff in onend(): several browsers (esp.
           mobile Chrome/Safari) end a "continuous" recognizer every
           few seconds by design even with no user action — this is a
           real, documented platform limitation, not a bug here, so
           onend() quietly restarts unless the user turned the toggle
           off, a real conversation now owns the mic, or restarts are
           happening too fast (>6 in 10s), in which case it backs off
           8s instead of hammering the mic in a tight loop.
         - isSupported() mirrors App.Voice.Recognition.isSupported() —
           same `window.SpeechRecognition || window.webkitSpeechRecognition`
           check. Where unsupported, or the browser has restricted
           background mic access, enable() reports why and the existing
           tap-to-talk mic button is completely unaffected — it was
           never touched by this module.

       Integration (the only existing code touched this sprint):
         - App.Voice.Bridge.startConversation() — one additive line:
           calls App.Voice.WakeWord.pauseForConversation() first, so
           whether a conversation starts via wake word OR the manual
           mic tap, the passive listener always yields the microphone
           rather than fighting the real session for it.
         - App.Voice.Bridge.stopConversation() — one additive line:
           calls App.Voice.WakeWord.resumeIfEnabled() last, so passive
           listening picks back up automatically once a conversation
           ends, only if the user still has the toggle on.
         - App.init() — one additive try/catch calling
           App.Voice.WakeWord.init() right after App.Voice.Bridge.init(),
           matching every other module's init pattern in this file. It
           only silently resumes listening if the user had wake word on
           AND mic permission is already granted — it never prompts for
           permission on page load.
         - The "🧩 AI Voice Assistant (Conversational)" card — two new
           rows added (Assistant's name text input; Wake word listening
           toggle + live status line), and "Wake Word" removed from the
           "reserved for future sprints" tag list since it's real now.
           No existing row, ID, class, or control in this card was
           removed, renamed, or reordered.
       No IDs, classes, navigation, save/entry functions, or any other
       existing voice/finance logic were removed or altered anywhere in
       this sprint.

       Honesty note (real technical limits, not glossed over): true
       always-on background wake-word listening — the phone screen off,
       tab backgrounded, browser fully suspended — is NOT reliably
       possible from a web page on iOS Safari or most mobile Chrome
       builds; the OS suspends page JS and/or the mic to save battery,
       same restriction every "Hey Siri"-style *web* app runs into (only
       native/OS-level assistants can truly listen while backgrounded).
       This feature works while Khaata's tab is open and in the
       foreground/active; if the browser pauses it after backgrounding,
       resuming the tap-to-talk mic button always works exactly as
       before — that fallback was never touched. The toggle is off by
       default and clearly labeled so battery/privacy tradeoffs are the
       user's explicit choice.

       All 4 parts of the Voice Command Brain build are now in place:
       Part 1 (App.VoiceBrain — navigate/add/question routing), Part 2
       (App.VoiceFollowUp — spoken clarifying questions), Part 3
       (App.VoiceQA — spoken financial Q&A), Part 4 (this sprint —
       custom name + optional wake-word activation).
     ------------------------------------------------------------------
     Sprint 39 — Voice Command Brain, Part 3 of 4 (App.VoiceQA — new)
       App version bumped: 1.33.0 -> 1.34.0
       Goal: let the user ASK financial questions by voice — "Is mahine
       kitna kharch hua?", "Sabse zyada kis category mein paisa gaya?",
       "Pichle mahine ke mukable is mahine kaisa chal raha hai?" — and
       get a real spoken + written answer computed from actual stored
       data. No invented numbers anywhere in this sprint.

       What already existed (checked first, reused as-is, never
       recomputed): App.Reports.Filter.range()/apply()/previousRange(),
       App.Reports.Builder.build(focus, txns, f) (the exact 13-focus
       report engine — 'overview' and 'category' foci are what this
       sprint calls), the global sum()/fmt()/startOfMonth()/endOfMonth()
       /dateInRange() helpers, and App.Voice.Synthesis.speak() (same TTS
       Sprint 19/37/38 already use). App.Reports/App.Analytics/App.Advisor
       calculation logic itself is untouched — this sprint only calls it.

       New module App.VoiceQA (pure query + presentation, no new
       finance math):
         - detect(text) — matches a transcript against 5 recognized
           financial data question types: this month's total expense,
           top spending category, income vs expense, savings rate, and
           this-month-vs-last-month comparison. Returns null for
           anything else (normal transaction/navigation phrasing, or a
           question App.VoiceQA doesn't recognize).
         - tryAnswer(text) — if detect() matches, builds the current
           (and where needed, previous) month's filter with
           App.Reports.Filter.range()/startOfMonth(-1)/endOfMonth(-1),
           gets the real transactions via App.Reports.Filter.apply(),
           and reads the answer straight out of
           App.Reports.Builder.build()'s kpis/rows — the identical data
           the Reports tab itself renders. Formats one short natural
           Hindi+English sentence, speaks it via App.Voice.Synthesis
           .speak(), and shows the same sentence as a chat bubble via
           the new App.Chat.showAssistantMessage() (below). Returns
           false (no side effects) if it doesn't recognize the
           question or the underlying report call throws, so the
           caller can fall through to the normal path unchanged.

       Integration (the only existing code touched this sprint):
         - App.VoiceBrain.route() — one additive check, same pattern as
           Sprint 38's Bridge hook: for a 'question' result, it now
           gives App.VoiceQA.tryAnswer() first refusal before falling
           through to the exact pre-Sprint-39 App.Chat.sendMessage()
           dispatch. 'add_transaction' results, and any 'question'
           App.VoiceQA doesn't recognize (e.g. genuinely open-ended
           questions still meant for App.OnlineAI), are completely
           unaffected — same call, same order, as Sprint 37 left it.
         - App.Chat — one small additive export, showAssistantMessage
           (text). It pushes/trims/persists/renders exactly like the
           existing private addMessage('ai', text) does, but does NOT
           notify aiMessageListeners — App.VoiceQA already speaks its
           own answer directly (same as App.VoiceFollowUp's speak()
           helper does), so routing this through the general
           auto-speak-every-reply listener too would speak the same
           sentence twice. No existing App.Chat function, ID, or
           behavior was renamed or altered.
       No IDs, classes, navigation, save/entry functions, or report/
       analytics calculation logic were removed or altered anywhere in
       this sprint.

       For Part 4 (say this in the next prompt so the next session
       finds it): App.VoiceQA.detect(text) / App.VoiceQA.tryAnswer(text)
       — the 5 recognized question-type ids are month_expense,
       top_category, income_vs_expense, savings_rate, compare_months.
       App.Chat.showAssistantMessage(text) is the new silent (no
       auto-speak-listener) chat-bubble export Part 4 can also reuse.
     ------------------------------------------------------------------
     Sprint 38 — Voice Command Brain, Part 2 of 4 (App.VoiceFollowUp — new)
       App version bumped: 1.32.0 -> 1.33.0
       Builds on the exact hook Sprint 37 called out for this part:
       App.VoiceBrain.classify()/route() results with needsFollowup:true
       (empty/filler transcripts, or a bare screen name said with no
       verb, e.g. "entry" or "report" on their own — see Sprint 37's
       VIEW_LABELS). Before this sprint those results were only logged
       ("needs_followup —") and nothing was said back or asked; a
       genuinely ambiguous voice command just went silent.
       New module App.VoiceFollowUp (pure orchestration, no new save/
       finance logic):
         - begin(voiceBrainResult) — speaks one clarifying question via
           the existing App.Voice.Synthesis.speak() (same TTS Sprint
           19/37 already use) and remembers what was asked (reason,
           target screen if any, retry count) for this conversation
           only.
         - handleAnswer(text) — the *next* transcript, when a question
           is pending, is treated as the answer instead of being
           reclassified as a brand-new command. Re-runs
           App.VoiceBrain.classify() on the answer (bare-screen-name
           case) or on the combined original+answer text (filler
           case): a decisive result is hand off to
           App.VoiceBrain.route() / the real App.Chat.sendMessage() —
           exactly the same dispatch Sprint 37 already does for every
           other command, never duplicated here. A still-ambiguous
           answer gets one more spoken retry (2 retries max, per the
           brief); after that it falls back gracefully by opening the
           real, existing manual Entry form (switchToView('entry'))
           so the user is never stuck waiting on a misheard mic.
         - isPending()/reset() — small state helpers; reset() is
           called from Bridge's startConversation()/stopConversation()
           so a stale question can never leak into a new session.
       Slot-by-slot tracking of amount/category/account/date for an
       actual income/expense entry is deliberately NOT reimplemented
       here — App.Chat's private session/fields/nextMissingStep()
       machinery (Sprints 16-25) already does real multi-turn slot-
       filling with retries, edits, and confirmation, and already
       receives every voice transcript App.VoiceBrain hands it (intent
       'add_transaction'). Saying "entry karna hai" already reaches
       that real engine unchanged via Sprint 37's existing routing (it
       isn't ambiguous to VoiceBrain — only a bare screen name like
       "entry" alone, or silence/filler, needs this sprint's new
       question at all), and it is the one that ultimately calls the
       real save function once every field is known. This sprint never
       calls any save/entry function directly — it only ever hands a
       resolved command to App.VoiceBrain.route() or App.Chat.sendMessage(),
       the exact same two dispatch paths every other voice command
       already uses.
       Confirmation/context tracking: reuses the existing App.
       Conversation.Context.push()/App.Conversation.Logger placeholder
       modules (Sprint 18) to record each question/answer turn for
       traceability, instead of adding a second parallel turn-history
       array. Nothing in App.Conversation.* was modified.
       Only existing code touched: App.Voice.Bridge. handleFinalTranscript()
       gained two small additive checks — (1) if App.VoiceFollowUp has a
       pending question, the transcript goes there instead of through
       classify() again; (2) if a fresh App.VoiceBrain.handle() result
       comes back with needsFollowup:true, App.VoiceFollowUp.begin() is
       now called instead of the result being silently dropped.
       startConversation()/stopConversation() each gained one additive
       App.VoiceFollowUp.reset() call. Every pre-Sprint-38 behavior
       (no App.VoiceFollowUp present, or no follow-up pending) is
       byte-for-byte unchanged. No IDs, classes, event listeners, or
       business logic elsewhere were removed, renamed, or altered.
       Covers two command types end-to-end, per the brief: adding an
       entry (bare "entry"/"add entry"/"new entry") and checking a
       report (bare "report"/"monthly report", "reports"/"ai reports")
       — both go through the identical generic bare-screen-name
       handling, since it's keyed off the same VIEW_LABELS map Sprint
       37 already built, not a per-screen special case.
       For Part 3 (say this in the next prompt so the next session
       finds it): App.VoiceFollowUp.begin(result) / .handleAnswer(text)
       / .isPending() / .reset(), plus the two-line hook in
       App.Voice.Bridge.handleFinalTranscript() that calls them. Part 3
       is expected to build on top of these without changing their
       signatures — e.g. richer slot-aware clarifying questions, or
       wiring real App.Conversation.Question NLG in place of the fixed
       templates this sprint uses.
